<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Unyanyembe</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1893 CE (approx.) · capital Tabora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Nyamwezi chiefdom of central Tanzania, whose town of Tabora became the main inland hub of the 19th-century caravan trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Unyanyembe was one of many Nyamwezi chiefdoms in the plateau of west-central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania</a>. From the early 19th century Nyamwezi porters and traders opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the coast, and in the 1840s Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili merchants</a> settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>, which grew into a commercial town under the chief Fundikira.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ntemi (chief) ruled with councils of elders and drew wealth from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slave caravans that passed between the coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>. Relations with the Arab traders were tense: in the 1860s chief Mnywasele was overthrown in a war in which the traders backed his rival. Explorers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-francis-burton">Burton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">Speke</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">Livingstone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Stanley</a> stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chief Isike resisted the Germans, and in 1893, when his fortress was stormed, he took his own life. Unyanyembe was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> became a colonial administrative and railway centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/unyanyembe.jpg" alt="Unyanyembe" width="320" height="452"><figcaption>Image: David Livingstone (1813-1873) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pocket_chronometer_loan_receipt_by_David_Livingstone,_Consul_Inner_Africa,_at_Zanzibar_Museum_-_Unyanyembe,_February,_1872.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1893 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tabora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fundikira, Isike</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nyamwezi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Unyanyembe chiefdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=unyanyembe" aria-label="Unyanyembe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5617 2312 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Unyanyembe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5937 2568L6087 2688"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6087 2688h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5937 2568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5937 2568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1846&amp;focus=unyanyembe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

